1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:02,860
He found a body 270 metres down.

2
00:00:03,090 --> 00:00:04,190
Then he went back for it.

3
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:05,650
Ten minutes down.

4
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:07,750
About ten hours of stops back up.

5
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:11,150
This is Boesmansgat, Bushman's Hole, South Africa.

6
00:00:11,510 --> 00:00:15,960
In 2004, Australian pilot Dave Shaw set a depth record here.

7
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:18,670
At the bottom, his torch found another diver.

8
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:20,130
Deon Dreyer.

9
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:23,320
Lost here in 1994, never recovered.

10
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:26,110
Shaw planned one more dive, to bring him home.

11
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:28,310
January 2005.

12
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:31,640
Don Shirley would meet him at 220 metres.

13
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:35,010
Shaw reached the body and began easing it into a bag.

14
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,980
This deep, dense gas makes every breath hard work.

15
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:39,350
His lines snagged.

16
00:00:39,370 --> 00:00:40,430
He worked harder.

17
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:42,320
Carbon dioxide built up.

18
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:44,900
He never reached 220.

19
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:48,740
Shirley searched deeper, and was struck by decompression sickness.

20
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:49,620
He survived.

21
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:52,010
Shaw's helmet camera recorded it all.

22
00:00:52,210 --> 00:00:55,390
Experts concluded carbon dioxide made him black out.

23
00:00:55,850 --> 00:00:58,450
Four days later, divers hauled up his lines.

24
00:00:58,830 --> 00:01:00,940
Two bodies came up, tangled together.

25
00:01:01,530 --> 00:01:04,650
Ten years after he was lost, Deon Dreyer came home.
